1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,782
Hi, I'm Junsu.

2
00:00:00,782 --> 00:00:03,686
This is Dock Risk, built for the RoadStar Hackathon.

3
00:00:03,686 --> 00:00:07,094
It catches the moment a dock delay kills a driver's next load

4
00:00:07,094 --> 00:00:09,720
while dispatch can still do something about it.

5
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:12,381
A truck waits at a dock.

6
00:00:12,381 --> 00:00:13,800
Two clocks are running.

7
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:14,603
One is money.

8
00:00:14,603 --> 00:00:17,133
Past two hours, detention starts billing.

9
00:00:17,133 --> 00:00:18,429
The other is the law.

10
00:00:18,429 --> 00:00:20,342
Waiting counts as on-duty time,

11
00:00:20,342 --> 00:00:23,120
so his legal hours drain while he sits still.

12
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,340
Here's the part nobody watches.

13
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:28,447
The second clock runs out first.

14
00:00:28,447 --> 00:00:31,410
In this scenario the next load dies at 12:53.

15
00:00:31,410 --> 00:00:33,648
Free time doesn't end until 13:30.

16
00:00:33,648 --> 00:00:35,030
Thirty-seven minutes.

17
00:00:35,030 --> 00:00:38,520
Every detention tool on the market wakes up at 13:30.

18
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:40,864
So here's the desk.

19
00:00:40,864 --> 00:00:41,661
Eight trucks,

20
00:00:41,661 --> 00:00:44,665
ranked by which one is about to become a problem.

21
00:00:44,665 --> 00:00:47,179
The first two columns are the two clocks.

22
00:00:47,179 --> 00:00:48,834
Time until detention bills,

23
00:00:48,834 --> 00:00:50,980
hours until he has to stop driving.

24
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:53,033
Open Driver84.

25
00:00:53,033 --> 00:00:54,899
He's been waiting two hours.

26
00:00:54,899 --> 00:00:57,633
Detention starts in thirty-seven minutes.

27
00:00:57,633 --> 00:01:00,500
He has one hour left to reach a legal stop.

28
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:02,997
And there's the verdict.

29
00:01:02,997 --> 00:01:05,092
The next load is already impossible.

30
00:01:05,092 --> 00:01:08,701
Release him right now and he's still forty-nine minutes short.

31
00:01:08,701 --> 00:01:12,019
A live 401 closure is adding road time to the same check.

32
00:01:13,099 --> 00:01:15,216
The driver confirms his check-in.

33
00:01:15,216 --> 00:01:15,816
One tap.

34
00:01:15,730 --> 00:01:17,077
That tap is evidence.

35
00:01:17,077 --> 00:01:19,514
It sets when the billing clock starts,

36
00:01:19,514 --> 00:01:21,759
and it's kept with who reported it.

37
00:01:22,933 --> 00:01:24,965
Dispatch looks for a relief driver.

38
00:01:24,965 --> 00:01:27,635
Candidates come back with reasons, not scores.

39
00:01:27,635 --> 00:01:29,783
Distance, arrival time, trailer type,

40
00:01:29,783 --> 00:01:32,280
and whether his hours cover the whole trip.

41
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:35,473
Drivers who can't make it are blocked, and it says why.

42
00:01:36,666 --> 00:01:38,017
One offer goes out.

43
00:01:38,017 --> 00:01:40,291
The driver accepts on his phone.

44
00:01:40,291 --> 00:01:41,570
The load is saved.

45
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:45,266
The original stop keeps every piece of its evidence.

46
00:01:46,399 --> 00:01:50,049
Now Driver84 is released, and the charge writes itself.

47
00:01:50,049 --> 00:01:51,707
Fifteen billable minutes.

48
00:01:51,707 --> 00:01:53,167
Eighteen seventy-five.

49
00:01:53,167 --> 00:01:54,759
Billed to the consignee.

50
00:01:55,899 --> 00:01:57,790
This is what makes it collectable.

51
00:01:57,790 --> 00:02:01,127
The amount, then the arithmetic with every rule spelled out,

52
00:02:01,127 --> 00:02:03,741
then the ledger each event and who reported it.

53
00:02:03,741 --> 00:02:06,021
When the evidence is thin, we don't bill.

54
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:09,079
It's drafted, flagged, and the packet says exactly why.

55
00:02:10,233 --> 00:02:12,467
We replayed the organizers' own export.

56
00:02:12,467 --> 00:02:15,675
Fifty-six days, ten thousand legs, real dock timestamps.

57
00:02:15,675 --> 00:02:18,769
About thirty-one thousand dollars a month of exposure.

58
00:02:18,769 --> 00:02:22,263
And the thirty-minute warning caught a hundred and fifteen of

59
00:02:22,263 --> 00:02:25,013
the hundred and twenty-six stops that went over.

60
00:02:26,133 --> 00:02:29,676
We trained on the first four weeks and scored on the last

61
00:02:29,676 --> 00:02:32,037
four, so that number is out of sample.

62
00:02:32,037 --> 00:02:34,337
We also named what we couldn't build.

63
00:02:34,337 --> 00:02:37,755
Axle weight isn't possible — that sheet has one column.

64
00:02:37,755 --> 00:02:39,434
This isn't a certified ELD.

65
00:02:39,434 --> 00:02:41,547
And we say exposure, not unbilled,

66
00:02:41,547 --> 00:02:44,033
because the file has no billing records.

67
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:47,159
Detention timers are table stakes.

68
00:02:47,159 --> 00:02:50,496
Connecting the dock clock to the next dispatch decision,

69
00:02:50,496 --> 00:02:52,700
early enough to act that's Dock Risk.

70
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:54,846
Watch the clock that runs out first.

71
00:02:54,846 --> 00:02:58,302
*"I watch the video to get context and then I use that for

72
00:02:58,302 --> 00:03:01,638
testing the submission."* *"The video becomes crucial in

73
00:03:01,638 --> 00:03:05,333
giving us our first indicator of how much time was invested."*

